    "knowledge_point": "Writing the Area Bounded by a Curve as a Definite Integral",
    "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13947210/assets/149fd953075c1d9909e036d4935ccd4f17ad1734d86bba4a70254468cd2035d5.png)\n\nFor the function $f(x)={x}^{2}-2x+2$ shown above, what is the expression for the area $A$ as a definite integral?",
    "worked_solution": "We want to express the area $A$ between the function $f(x)={x}^{2}-2x+2$ and the $x$-axis, between $x=0$ and $x=3.$\n\nThis can be expressed as the definite integral of $f(x)={x}^{2}-2x+2$ between the lower limit $x=0$ and the upper limit $x=3,$ as follows:\n\n$$\nA={\\int }_{0}^{3}({x}^{2}-2x+2)\\,\\text{d}x\n$$",
